(tuto2)=
# Your first workflow

This tutorial will teach you how to chain functionalities to create a simple workflow.
Based on a real CFD case, the objectives are to demonstrate how Maia simplifies distributed
mesh operations and to provide a solid foundation for creating your own parallel CGNS workflows.

Our use case involves a Rotor37 geometry mesh.
The input mesh is available in different sizes; please download one from the table below.
The illustrations on this page were created using the medium-sized mesh.


[rotor_37_small.cgns]: https://github.com/onera/Maia/releases/download/v1.8/rotor37_small.cgns
[rotor_37_medium.cgns]: https://github.com/onera/Maia/releases/download/v1.8/rotor37_medium.cgns
[rotor_37_large.cgns]: https://github.com/onera/Maia/releases/download/v1.8/rotor37_large.cgns

|                   | Light                 | Medium                 | Large                 |
|-------------------|-----------------------|------------------------|-----------------------|
| Initial cell size | ~60K                  | ~200K                  | ~21M                  |
| File size         | 6 MiB                 | 16 MiB                 | 1 GiB                 |
| (Final cell size) | ~2M                   | ~7M                    | ~762M                 |
| Download link     | [rotor_37_small.cgns] | [rotor_37_medium.cgns] | [rotor_37_large.cgns] |

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [remove-cell, no-parallel]
# This create a symlink of the input file in current directory, for automatic execution
SRC = '/stck/jcoulet/Public/maia_training/MESHES/rotor37_medium_elt.cgns'
import os
if not os.path.exists('rotor37_medium.cgns'):
    os.symlink(SRC, 'rotor37_medium.cgns')
```

Then, ensure you have access to a valid installation of maia and create
an empty file {file}`02_workflow.py`. 
You will complete this file as you work through the tutorial; look out for
instructions preceded by the symbol ✏️.

```{tip}
During the experimentation phase, we advise you to use a light mesh and a low number of processes, especially if you are displaying data.
Once the script is ready, you can use more processes and move to larger meshes.
```

## Objective and plan

The aim of the workflow is to recombine a complete 360° mesh from the input angular section,
with a data field initialized on it:

```{glue:figure} target_fig
:align: center
:width: 85%

Initial (left) and expected final (right) mesh, colored from `VelocityY` field. Black lines
show the initial angular section position.
```

We precise that we expect to have a single `Zone_t` node on the 360° mesh.

👉 Before starting to code, let's see what will be needed. Open the {ref}`user_manual`
and answer the following questions:

❓ Which function(s) you will need to use ?

*Hint : try to search 360 or duplicate in the search bar* 

```{toggle}
The main functions you are going to use are :
- {func}`~maia.algo.dist.duplicate_from_rotation_jns_to_360` to duplicate the input mesh,
- {func}`~maia.algo.dist.merge_zones` (or equivalent) to merge the resulting zones in a single one.

In addition, you will need some `PT` functions to create and store the initial field, as well as
the standard `maia.io` functions.
```

❓ Will you need to work on a partioned view of the input mesh ?

```{toggle}
All the functions mentionned above operate on a distributed tree. Thus, partioning the
tree will not be necessary.
```

❓ When should you create the initial field ?

```{toggle}
You can create the initial field anytime in your workflow, but it is probably more
efficient to do it before the duplication (since there will be more cells
to compute after).
```

👉 Last step: it is a good practice to quickly check the input mesh, *eg* with `maia_print_tree`:


```{code-cell} python
:tags: [remove-input, no-parallel]
import subprocess
_ = subprocess.run(
    ["maia_print_tree", "rotor37_medium.cgns", "--depth=3"],
)
```

❓ Is the input mesh suitable for the functions you plan to call ?

```{toggle}
❌️ No! The input mesh is described by standard elements, but the function
{func}`~maia.algo.dist.merge_zones` only applies to polyedric elements.
Thus, a mesh conversion into polyedric (`NGON_n`) elements will be needed.
```

👉 All these informations allow us to propose the following plan:

```{toggle}
1. Load mesh
2. Convert into polyedric tree
3. Initialize fields
4. Duplicate 
5. Merge zones
```

## Development

### Prelude

✏️ As in {ref}`first tutorial <tuto1>`, get the communicator
from `mpi4py` package and import the modules that we will use: `maia` and `maia.pytree`.

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
from mpi4py import MPI
comm = MPI.COMM_WORLD

import maia
import maia.pytree as PT
```


### File reading



✏️ Read the CGNS file of your choice from the disk using the main file reading function : 
{func}`maia.io.file_to_dist_tree`.

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
:mystnb:
:  code_prompt_show: "Show solution"
:  code_prompt_hide: "Hide solution"
tree = maia.io.file_to_dist_tree('rotor37_medium.cgns', comm)
```

### Connectivity conversion

👉 Search in the {ref}`user_manual` the relevant function to convert the
elements description from standard elements to polyedric (`NGON_n`) elements.

✏️ Call the function and check it worked by printing the
{func}`~maia.pytree.Element.Type` of `Elements_t` nodes.

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
elt_kinds = set(PT.Element.Type(e) for e in PT.iter_nodes_from_label(tree, 'Elements_t'))
if comm.Get_rank() == 0:
  print(f"Elements type was {elt_kinds}")

maia.algo.dist.convert_elements_to_ngon(tree, comm)

elt_kinds = set(PT.Element.Type(e) for e in PT.iter_nodes_from_label(tree, 'Elements_t'))
if comm.Get_rank() == 0:
  print(f"Elements type are now {elt_kinds}")
```

```{tip}
The {ref}`pt_inspect` page lists various functions to easily get
data related to the most frequent node labels.
```

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [remove-cell]
# Save for visualization
maia.io.dist_tree_to_file(tree, 'rotor37_medium_ng.cgns', comm)
```

### Field initialization

In order to illustrate the ability of the duplication to move the vectorial fields
together with the mesh, we are going to create a vectorial initial field in the input zone.

This will made you use some functions of {ref}`pytree_module`.

✏️ Get the (cartesian) coordinates $x$,$y$, and $z$ of the input zone and use it to compute
the following vertex located velocity :

```python
vx = α 
vy = -α*sin(Θ)     where α = sqrt(2)/2
vz =  α*cos(Θ)           Θ = atan(z/y)
```

which corresponds to the uniform field $(0, \alpha, \alpha)$ in the cylindric coordinates
$(\vec{e_{r}}, \vec{e_{\theta}}, \vec{e_{x}})$ (since $\vec{e_x}$ is the revolution axis of the mesh).

```{attention}
You need to create arrays locally sized as $x$, $y$ and $z$. Use one of the ndarray methods to get this size.
```

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
:mystnb:
:  code_prompt_show: "Show solution"
:  code_prompt_hide: "Hide solution"
zone = PT.find_node_from_label(tree, 'Zone_t') # OK because tree has only one zone
cx, cy, cz = PT.Zone.coordinates(zone)

import numpy as np
α = np.sqrt(2)/2
Θ = np.arctan(cz/cy)
vx = np.full(len(cx), α)
vy = -α * np.sin(Θ)
vz =  α * np.cos(Θ)
```

✏️ Add the fields in a FlowSolution container. Be carefull to end the names
by X,Y and Z to have it considered as a vectorial field later.

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell, remove-output]
:mystnb:
:  code_prompt_show: "Show solution"
:  code_prompt_hide: "Hide solution"
PT.new_FlowSolution('FlowSolution',
                    loc='Vertex',
                    fields={
                      'VelocityX' : vx,
                      'VelocityY' : vy,
                      'VelocityZ' : vz},
                    parent=zone)
```

```{note}
An alternative approach would have be to convert the mesh into cylindrical coordinates,
then to create the FlowSolution with the uniform field $(0, \alpha, \alpha)$ (using this time
R, Theta, and Z suffixes) before moving back the mesh to cartesian coordinates.
Try it if you want!
```

### 360° duplication

👉 It is time to apply the duplication operation with {func}`~maia.algo.dist.duplicate_from_rotation_jns_to_360`.

❓ This function takes as an input the paths of the `GridConnectivity_t` nodes that define
the periodic transformation. What is wrong with this mesh ?

```{toggle}
Our mesh has no `GridConnectivity_t` nodes ! Apparently, the meshing tool did not store it and we need to
recover it. Congratulations if you anticipated it at the begining of this tutorial 🏅️
```

#### Recovering GridConnectivity_t nodes

In fact, the concerned boundary faces are stored in two `BC_t` nodes : `perleft` and `perright`; it is 'only'
the 1-to-1 mapping which is missing.

✏️ Read the documentation of {func}`~maia.algo.dist.connect_1to1_families` and call it to rebuild the 1to1 connection
between these two subsets.

```{tip}
The rotation angle to go from perleft subset to perright subset is `2*pi/36`
```

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
:mystnb:
:  code_prompt_show: "Show solution"
:  code_prompt_hide: "Hide solution"
a = 2*np.pi / 36
maia.algo.dist.connect_1to1_families(tree,
                                     ('perleft', 'perright'), 
                                     comm,
                                     periodic={'rotation_angle' : np.array([a, 0, 0])})
```

✏️ Then check the presence of `GridConnectivity_t` nodes in the tree and note their path.

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
:mystnb:
:  code_prompt_show: "Show solution"
:  code_prompt_hide: "Hide solution"
if comm.Get_rank() == 0:
  PT.print_tree(tree, max_depth=4)
```

#### Effective duplication

Now that tree has periodic joins, we can call the duplication function.

✏️ Use the paths of the `GridConnectivity_t` created nodes to call the duplication function.
    
```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
:mystnb:
:  code_prompt_show: "Show solution"
:  code_prompt_hide: "Hide solution"

zone_paths = ["Base/Rotor"]
left_jns   = ["Base/Rotor/ZoneGridConnectivity/perleft_0"]
right_jns  = ["Base/Rotor/ZoneGridConnectivity/perright_0"]

maia.algo.dist.duplicate_from_rotation_jns_to_360(tree,
                                           zone_paths,
                                           jn_paths_for_dupl = (left_jns, right_jns),
                                           comm = comm)
```

❓ How many `Zone_t` nodes are registered in the tree after the duplication ?

```{toggle}
The tree has 36 zones after the duplication operation
(since the periodicity angle was $\frac{2\pi}{36}$).
You can check this with `len(PT.get_all_Zone_t(tree))`.
```

### Merging zones

As you noticed, the tree has now several physical zones (due to the duplication).
Sometimes it can be useful to merge these zones, which are still connected throught
1to1 matching joins, into a single one, for example:
- to call a tool that does not manage multiblock meshes (e.g. mesh adaptation)
- to avoid interface management in the solver.

Search in the {ref}`documentation <user_manual>` the function to call to merge the zones.

✏️ Call the function and check again the number of ``Zone_t`` nodes in the output tree.

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [hide-cell]
:mystnb:
:  code_prompt_show: "Show solution"
:  code_prompt_hide: "Hide solution"

maia.algo.dist.merge_connected_zones(tree, comm)

if comm.Get_rank() == 0:
  print(f"The number zones is now {len(PT.get_all_Zone_t(tree))}")
```

### Conclusion

Our preprocessing workflow is completed 🚀!

This is what the final tree looks like:

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [scroll_output, hide-output]
if comm.Get_rank() == 0:
  PT.print_tree(tree)
```

👉 If you want to create a visualisation, you can save it using {func}`~maia.io.dist_tree_to_file`.
Be careful with the large mesh, as it requires more than 70 GiB of disk space.

You can also download the final script {download}`02_pre.py`.

```{code-cell} python
:tags: [remove-cell]
# This snipper prepares tree for illustration (write only surfaces)
ptree = maia.factory.partition_dist_tree(tree, comm, data_transfer='ALL')

for bc in PT.get_nodes_from_label(ptree, 'BC_t'):
  PT.new_FamilyName('ALLBCS', as_additional='AdditionalFamilyName', parent=bc)

ext = maia.algo.part.extract_part_from_family(ptree, 'ALLBCS', comm, containers_name='ALL')
dext = maia.factory.recover_dist_tree(ext, comm, data_transfer='ALL')
PT.rm_nodes_from_name(dext, 'ZoneBC')
maia.algo.dist.convert_ngon_to_elements(dext, comm)
maia.io.dist_tree_to_file(dext, 'output_rotor37_med.cgns', comm)
```


